imi et al. 2018","Wallace et al. (2019)","HotFlip","Shin et al., 2020","Mehrabi et al. (2022)","Zou et al. (2023)","OpenAI 2023","UAT and AutoPrompt","Jones et al. 2023","#","Wei et al. (2023)","DAN","AIM","it can understand","Wei et al. (2023)","Greshake et al. (2023)","#","Wallace et al. (2019)","QuizBowl QA dataset","Ziegler et al. (2022)","Wallace et al. (2019)","BERT-Attack","Xu et al. 2021","BAD dataset","Tensorflow dataset","red-teaming dataset","Ganguli, et al. 2022","GPT-4","DALL-E 3","#","Perez et al. 2022","A2C","BAD (Bot Adversarial Dialogue) dataset","nucleus sampling","Casper et al. (2023)","Perez et al. (2022)","here","here","here","Mehrabi et al. 2023","in-context learning","Perez et al. 2022","Schramowski et al. 2022","https://github.com/notAI-tech/NudeNet)","Perspective API","#","#","Madry et al. 2017","#","Xie et al. 2023","Jain et al. 2023","White-box attacks","UAT-LM","Jain et al. 2023","#","#","â€œTowards Deep Learning Models Resistant to Adversarial Attacksâ€","â€œSemantically equivalent adversarial rules for debugging NLP modelsâ€","â€œGradient-based adversarial attacks against text transformersâ€","â€œHotFlip: White-Box Adversarial Examples for Text Classificationâ€","â€œUniversal Adversarial Triggers for Attacking and Analyzing NLP.â€","code","â€œRobust Conversational Agents against Imperceptible Toxicity Triggers.â€","â€œUniversal and Transferable Adversarial Attacks on Aligned Language Models.â€","â€œRLPrompt: Optimizing Discrete Text Prompts with Reinforcement Learning.â€","â€œIs BERT Really Robust? A Strong Baseline for Natural Language Attack on Text Classification and Entailment.â€","â€œBERT-Attack: Adversarial Attack Against BERT Using BERT.â€","\"TextAttack: A Framework for Adversarial Attacks, Data Augmentation, and Adversarial Training in NLP.\"","â€œBot-Adversarial Dialogue for Safe Conversational Agents.â€","â€œAdversarial training for high-stakes reliability.â€","â€œRed Teaming Language Models to Reduce Harms: Methods, Scaling Behaviors, and Lessons Learned.â€","â€œRed Teaming Language Models with Language Models.â€","â€œRed Teaming Language Models to Reduce Harms: Methods, Scaling Behaviors, and Lessons Learned.â€","â€œFLIRT: Feedback Loop In-context Red Teaming.â€","â€œExplore, Establish, Exploit: Red Teaming Language Models from Scratch.â€","â€œDefending ChatGPT against Jailbreak Attack via Self-Reminder.â€","â€œAutomatically Auditing Large Language Models via Discrete Optimization.â€","â€œCompromising Real-World LLM-Integrated Applications with Indirect Prompt Injection.â€","â€œBaseline Defenses for Adversarial Attacks Against Aligned Language Models.â€","â€œJailbroken: How Does LLM Safety Training Fail?â€","â€œEDA: Easy data augmentation techniques for boosting performance on text classification tasks.â€","www.jailbreakchat.com","â€œYou can use GPT-4 to create prompt injections against GPT-4â€","Nlp","Language-Model","Safety","Adversarial Attacks","Robustness","Redteam"],"chroma:document":"Then $B < kL$ token substitution candidates ${\\mathbf{t}^{(1)}, \\dots, \\mathbf{t}^{(B)}}$ are selected out of $kL$ options at random and the one with best loss (i.e. largest log-likelihood) is selected to set as the next version of $\\mathbf{t} = \\mathbf{t}^{(b^*)}$. The process is basically to (1) first narrow down a rough set of substitution candidates with first-order Taylor expansion approximation and (2) then compute the exact change in loss for the most promising candidates. Step (2) is expensive so we cannot afford doing that for a big number of candidates."}␀␀␀,"i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","i","b","i","b","b","i","b","i","i","i","b","b","i","i","i","i","i","b","i","i"],"filetype":"text/html"}␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀